# =============================================================================
# THESE ARE PUBLIC DEMO KEYS. They are identical on every machine that runs
# `supabase start`, they are published in Supabase's own documentation, and
# they are NEVER valid in production. They are committed on purpose.
#
# A deployed environment (APP_ENV staging or production) refuses to start if
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL points at localhost or 127.0.0.1
# (src/lib/supabase/env.ts).
# =============================================================================
#
# Copy to .env.local (gitignored), then `npm run db:start`. See
# supabase/LOCAL.md. `npx supabase status` prints the same values.

# Absolute origin, no trailing slash. Drives metadataBase, every canonical URL,
# the JSON-LD @id values, robots.txt and sitemap.xml.
#
# BUILD-TIME ONLY. NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the output by
# `next build`; they are not read again when the server starts. Every page here
# is statically generated, so if this variable is missing during the build, the
# http://localhost:3000 fallback in src/content/site.ts is compiled into every
# canonical URL and every JSON-LD @id. Setting it in the runtime environment
# afterwards will NOT fix that - it takes another build.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The local stack's API gateway (supabase/config.toml, [api] port).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# The local stack's demo anon key. Public: RLS decides what it reaches, and
# anon can SELECT nothing (supabase/README.md). No fallback in code: if this or
# the URL is missing, the Supabase client throws when it is constructed and
# names it. A build that never constructs one does not need either.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJleHAiOjE5ODM4MTI5OTZ9.CRXP1A7WOeoJeXxjNni43kdQwgnWNReilDMblYTn_I0

# APP_ENV is deliberately NOT set here, and must never be added to this file.
#
# It says which environment this is (local | staging | production) and is set
# only in the deploy environment. Unset means local. It, not NODE_ENV, is
# what arms the localhost refusal: `next build` sets NODE_ENV=production on
# every machine, and a local production build (the one check:seo runs
# against) must keep working with the local stack's URL.
